Let the split-KV verify attention read vLLM's per-tensor fp8 KV cache (`--kv-cache-dtype fp8`,
sm89 and up), and hook it into the Triton backend beside the int8 per-token-head path.

Why: on a 4090, `--attention-backend TRITON_ATTN --kv-cache-dtype fp8` with the drafter on
Triton too (`--speculative-config '{..., "attention_backend": "TRITON_ATTN"}'`) is the only
0.28 configuration that keeps FULL CUDA graphs with speculative decoding on the fp8 pool
(FlashInfer's spec-decode support is single-token decode only, so it drops to PIECEWISE). But
vLLM's unified Triton attention will not split the KV sequence for a multi-token verify, so
that route decays with context: DFlash2 fp8 at 120k, greedy, fresh prefill, 256 out, median
of 3 on the 4090 went 71.4 / 50.9 / 35.4 tok/s at 24k / 49k / 88k tokens against 84.4 /
81.7 / 83.5 for stock fp8 FlashInfer with PIECEWISE (issue #87). The split-KV kernel here
already holds bf16 flat with depth (113 tok/s at 24k and 49k on the same card, production);
this patch gives it the fp8 cache.

What changes:

1. v1/attention/ops/spec_decode_attn.py gains an FP8 path beside QUANT. The Triton backend's
   per-tensor fp8 cache is [num_blocks, block_size, Hkv, D] float8_e4m3fn with one float32
   scale per layer tensor (layer._k_scale / layer._v_scale). fp8 e4m3 to bf16 is exact (3
   mantissa bits into 8), and the scalar scales fold in after each dot the way the int8
   per-token scales do, so the only new rounding is the bf16 dot the kernel already does:
       s   = (q . k_fp8->bf16) * k_scale
       acc = (p . v_fp8->bf16) * v_scale
   The descales arrive as one-element tensors and are loaded once per program. vLLM also
   quantizes the QUERY to fp8 on this cache path (layer._q_scale), so the kernel takes the
   query's descale too and undoes it once at load; the gate's first version excluded that
   case and the server ran the stock kernel (issue 87, the null row on 2026-09-09).

2. v1/attention/backends/triton_attn.py routes FP8_PER_TENSOR caches (bf16 query, so no
   q_descale) through the same gate as int8, passing the layer's scalar scales; one log line
   per layer marks the path engaged. flash_attn.py's _spec_attn_run carries the descales.

Measured (bench/test_spec_decode_fp8.py, 4090, 13 shapes to 90k tokens, q 1-64, plus six
shapes with the fp8 query): worst max|fp8 kernel - fp32 reference on the dequantized cache| =
0.0084, below the bf16 kernel's error on the same values in every row. Per attention layer, batch 1, us: kv 90k q 8, fp8 628 /
bf16 615 / FA2 5057; kv 25k q 16, fp8 378 / bf16 397 / FA2 1431. A deliberately wrong key
descale turns all 13 rows red. Server, DFlash2 fp8 at 120k on the 4090 (greedy, 256 out, fresh prefill, median of 3, 24k /
49k / 88k tokens): the Triton route with this kernel decodes 108.7 / 88.6 / 81.0 tok/s with
FULL graphs, against 71.4 / 50.9 / 35.4 without it and 84.4 / 81.7 / 83.5 for stock fp8
FlashInfer (PIECEWISE); production bf16 on the same kernel does 113 / 114 at 24k / 49k.

Order: applies on top of spec-decode-attn.patch (with the issue #86 int64 cast), spec-decode-int8-kv.patch
and spec-decode-scratch-within-budget.patch (glob order does that; this name sorts after them).
Not for sm86: Triton has no native fp8 conversion there and the backend already refuses fp8
below SM89, so the 3090 keeps the int8 route. Apply from the repo root:
  patch -p1 -d venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vllm < patches/triton-spec-attn-fp8-kv.patch

